[32m+[m[32mRead-ahead threads[m
[32m+[m[32m------------------[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe threads above compress and decompress: they split the blocks of one chunk between them, and each chunk is done[m
[32m+[m[32mbefore the next one starts.  Reading a slice of a frame on disk is a different job.  Each block that is not in the[m
[32m+[m[32mpage cache yet has to wait for the storage, and on a network file system every such wait is a round trip to the[m
[32m+[m[32mserver.  `blosc2_set_prefetch(nthreads, max_ahead, piece_size)` (or the `BLOSC_PREFETCH` environment variable) starts[m
[32m+[m[32mseparate read-ahead threads for that.  When a slice starts (`b2nd_get_slice_cbuffer`, `b2nd_get_slice_cbuffer_into`,[m
[32m+[m[32m`b2nd_get_slice`, `b2nd_get_orthogonal_selection`, `blosc2_schunk_get_slice_buffer`), up to `nthreads` of them read[m
[32m+[m[32mall the chunks it needs (that are not cached already) in pieces of at most `piece_size` bytes, in parallel and in the[m
[32m+[m[32morder the slice decompresses them, at most `max_ahead` bytes ahead of it; the slice waits for each chunk only right[m
[32m+[m[32mbefore decompressing it.  Of a chunk[m
[32m+[m[32mthat a slice of an array covers only in part, only the blocks the slice needs are read (after the chunk's header,[m
[32m+[m[32mwhich says where they are: one round trip more when it is not cached).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mRead-ahead threads spend most of their time waiting for the storage, but reading is not free: the kernel copies the[m
[32m+[m[32mdata, and it spends more CPU per byte the more threads read the same file at once.  Which settings to use:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m* 4 threads and the default 1 MiB pieces, on network file systems (NFS, SMB, ...) and on local disks.  On NFS,[m
[32m+[m[32m  cold patches of 3D images got 3-5x faster this way (8 threads: 10-25% faster still, for 20-40% more CPU per read);[m
[32m+[m[32m  on NVMe disks 1.5-3x.[m
[32m+[m[32m* Less CPU on local disks: fewer threads (1-2) or larger pieces (4-8 MiB).  On one NVMe system that kept the speed at[m
[32m+[m[32m  up to 40% less CPU; on another it cost 10-30% of the speed.[m
[32m+[m[32m* Many reader processes or threads at once: they already read in parallel.  When they keep all cores busy, the[m
[32m+[m[32m  kernel time read-ahead adds can cost more than it saves, and fewer threads (1-2) or none can be better.  In our[m
[32m+[m[32m  tests on NFS (3D images, 24 to 96 reader processes on 64 hardware threads), 4 threads were the fastest setting or[m
[32m+[m[32m  within 10% of it.[m
[32m+[m[32m* GPFS (IBM Storage Scale) needs no setting of its own.  GPFS caches what ``read()`` reads in its own pagepool, not in[m
[32m+[m[32m  the page cache, so for memory-mapped frames (``BLOSC2_IO_FILESYSTEM_MMAP``) reading ahead would not spare the slice[m
[32m+[m[32m  its page faults, and GPFS page faults are expensive.  There the threads populate the mapping instead[m
[32m+[m[32m  (``madvise(MADV_POPULATE_READ)``, Linux >= 5.14): 3D patches got 1.10-1.34x faster than with read-ahead reading[m
[32m+[m[32m  (cached and cold).  On NFS and local disks populating was slower for cold reads (up to 1.3x), so it is used on GPFS[m
[32m+[m[32m  only (recognized in builds with glibc >= 2.28); ``BLOSC_PREFETCH_POPULATE=1`` (``0``) uses it for all[m
[32m+[m[32m  memory-mapped frames (for none).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mHow many read-ahead threads run: up to `nthreads` for each slice being read, per process.  A process that reads with[m
[32m+[m[32mone thread has `nthreads` of them; one whose 8 threads read at the same time, up to 8 x `nthreads` (at most 256 in[m
[32m+[m[32mall); 8 processes that read with one thread each, `nthreads` each.  The decompression threads above do not count: a[m
[32m+[m[32mread with 8 decompression threads and `nthreads` = 4 has 4 read-ahead threads, which fetch the next chunks while the 8[m
[32m+[m[32mdecompress the current one.  So the two kinds are independent: a program that runs many reader processes or threads[m
[32m+[m[32mcan keep one decompression thread each (`nthreads=1`) and still read ahead in each.  Read-ahead threads run until[m
[32m+[m[32m`blosc2_destroy()`, each keeping a buffer of up to `piece_size` bytes.  Read-ahead is off by default and has no effect[m
[32m+[m[32mon Windows.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThreads and fork()[m
[32m+[m[32m------------------[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThreads do not survive `fork()`: a child process has none of its parent's compression, decompression or read-ahead[m
[32m+[m[32mthreads and starts its own on first use, also for the contexts and arrays it inherited.  While another thread is inside[m
[32m+[m[32ma call that uses the global context (`blosc2_compress()`, `blosc2_decompress()`, `blosc2_set_nthreads()`, ...), `fork()`[m
[32m+[m[32mwaits for that call to return.  As with any library, a context or frame that another thread was using at the moment of[m
[32m+[m[32mthe `fork()` must not be used in the child.[m
