[32m+[m[32mChanges from 3.4.1 to 3.5.0[m
[32m+[m[32m===========================[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThis minor release introduces new APIs and delivers major performance enhancements,[m
[32m+[m[32mparticularly for frame slicing, serialization, read-ahead prefetching, and I/O[m
[32m+[m[32mhandling on local disks and NFS, alongside bug and leak fixes.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m* Open frames through Windows file symlinks even when CRT path metadata reports[m
[32m+[m[32m  a zero size; validate and cache the size of the opened target instead.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Read-ahead for slices of frames on disk**, off by default: with[m
[32m+[m[32m  `blosc2_set_prefetch(nthreads, max_ahead, piece_size)` (or the[m
[32m+[m[32m  `BLOSC_PREFETCH` environment variable), `b2nd_get_slice_cbuffer()`,[m
[32m+[m[32m  `b2nd_get_slice()`, `b2nd_get_orthogonal_selection()` and[m
[32m+[m[32m  `blosc2_schunk_get_slice_buffer()` read the chunks a slice needs and that[m
[32m+[m[32m  are not in the page cache with a few background threads, in pieces of at[m
[32m+[m[32m  most `piece_size` bytes (1 MiB by default) and in the order they decompress[m
[32m+[m[32m  them, at most `max_ahead` bytes ahead, and decompress each chunk as soon as[m
[32m+[m[32m  it is in.  Of chunks that a slice of an array covers only in part, only the[m
[32m+[m[32m  blocks it needs are read (after the chunk's header).  Without it every block[m
[32m+[m[32m  of a cold slice is a synchronous read of at most the kernel's read-ahead[m
[32m+[m[32m  window, which on network file systems is one round trip each.  Cold patches[m
[32m+[m[32m  of 3D images on NFS got 3-5x faster, on a local NVMe disk 1.5-3x.  See[m
[32m+[m[32m  `README_THREADED.rst` for which settings to use.  POSIX only (Linux, macOS);[m
[32m+[m[32m  a no-op on Windows.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **New `blosc2_schunk_to_buffer_alloc()`**: serializes a super-chunk into a[m
[32m+[m[32m  contiguous frame in memory that the caller provides through a callback,[m
[32m+[m[32m  called once with the exact length (e.g. the final `bytes` object of a[m
[32m+[m[32m  binding), which saves copying the frame afterwards.  Unless the super-chunk[m
[32m+[m[32m  already is an in-memory frame, the frame is written there in place.  It is[m
[32m+[m[32m  the same frame that `blosc2_schunk_to_buffer()` returns.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **New `blosc2_schunk_get_chunk_range()`**: where a chunk of a super-chunk in[m
[32m+[m[32m  a contiguous frame on disk is in its file (offset and size), from the frame[m
[32m+[m[32m  header and the chunk index, so that callers can read or prefetch the chunks[m
[32m+[m[32m  they need themselves.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **The shuffle filter no longer slows down 1-byte types**: for[m
[32m+[m[32m  `typesize == 1` (uint8, int8, bool, ...) a byte shuffle is the identity, but[m
[32m+[m[32m  the SIMD dispatchers fell back to the generic routine, which copied every[m
[32m+[m[32m  byte on its own.  With SHUFFLE in the pipeline (the python-blosc2 default)[m
[32m+[m[32m  this made compression of uint8 data about 2x and decompression about 3.5x[m
[32m+[m[32m  slower than without a filter.  The filter pipeline now skips a byte shuffle[m
[32m+[m[32m  of 1-byte elements, and `blosc2_shuffle()`/`blosc2_unshuffle()` copy the[m
[32m+[m[32m  block in that case.  The compressed format is unchanged.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **The documentation of `blosc2_schunk_update_chunk()` states who owns the[m
[32m+[m[32m  chunk**, in the words already used for appending and inserting: with[m
[32m+[m[32m  `copy=false` the super-chunk owns the chunk only after a successful call;[m
[32m+[m[32m  after a failed one the caller keeps it and must free it.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Faster b2nd slices**: `b2nd_get_slice_cbuffer()` and[m
[32m+[m[32m  `b2nd_set_slice_cbuffer()` decompress (compress) every chunk that lies in[m
[32m+[m[32m  the slice and forms one run of the buffer straight into (from) the caller's[m
[32m+[m[32m  buffer, and a read no longer zeroes the part of the buffer that the slice[m
[32m+[m[32m  fills.  For chunks read or written in part, the worker threads copy each[m
[32m+[m[32m  block between the buffer and the block they decode (encode), instead of the[m
[32m+[m[32m  calling thread going through a chunk-sized scratch.  On Linux, a fresh read[m
[32m+[m[32m  buffer of 4 MB or more that gets transparent huge pages is prefaulted from[m
[32m+[m[32m  the calling thread, as threads faulting on the same huge page together spend[m
[32m+[m[32m  most of the read in the kernel.  With 28 threads, reading a 315 MB array[m
[32m+[m[32m  went from 41 to 24 ms, a random 128^3 patch from 1.9 to 1.0 ms, and writing[m
[32m+[m[32m  the whole array from 94 to 75 ms.  `blosc2_schunk_get_slice_buffer()`[m
[32m+[m[32m  decompresses large parts of chunks with the context threads (half of an[m
[32m+[m[32m  8 MB chunk: 0.53 to 0.16 ms).[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Faster frame copies, serialization and saves**: `blosc2_schunk_copy()`[m
[32m+[m[32m  into a new contiguous frame (behind `blosc2_schunk_to_buffer()` of anything[m
[32m+[m[32m  but an in-memory frame, `blosc2_schunk_to_file()` and `b2nd_copy()` to a[m
[32m+[m[32m  file) writes every chunk once, straight from the source, and the offsets,[m
[32m+[m[32m  header and trailer once at the end, instead of appending the chunks one by[m
[32m+[m[32m  one; the frames are byte-identical.  Serializing a 315 MB array (241 MB[m
[32m+[m[32m  compressed) went from 103 to 54 ms (15 ms with[m
[32m+[m[32m  `blosc2_schunk_to_buffer_alloc()`), saving it to a file from 57 to 45 ms,[m
[32m+[m[32m  the raw write speed of the disk.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Writes to on-disk frames read chunk headers only**: appending, inserting,[m
[32m+[m[32m  updating and deleting a chunk read whole chunks (a reference chunk for one[m
[32m+[m[32m  flags byte, the replaced chunk for its sizes, twice for an update); they now[m
[32m+[m[32m  read the 32-byte chunk headers.  Writing a whole 315 MB array on disk takes[m
[32m+[m[32m  20-31% less time on local disks and 38% less on NFS.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Chunk-sized temporaries are reused**: b2nd slice reads and writes on[m
[32m+[m[32m  frames, and the chunk reads of on-disk frames, take their buffers from a[m
[32m+[m[32m  per-thread cache instead of a malloc/free per call, whose memory glibc gave[m
[32m+[m[32m  back to the kernel each time, so that it was page-faulted in again: 16 slice[m
[32m+[m[32m  writes on an on-disk array went from 71 to 38 ms.  The cache holds at most[m
[32m+[m[32m  four buffers of 64 KB to 32 MB and 64 MB per thread; it is freed when the[m
[32m+[m[32m  thread exits and, for the calling thread, by `blosc2_destroy()`.  Not on[m
[32m+[m[32m  Windows.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Fewer opens on NFS**: opening a frame file with the default backend keeps[m
[32m+[m[32m  the handle that read its header as the cached reader, and creating one keeps[m
[32m+[m[32m  the creating handle as the cached writer (and reads through a duplicate of[m
[32m+[m[32m  it).  Opening costs one OPEN/CLOSE round trip instead of two, creating an[m
[32m+[m[32m  array 4 RPCs instead of 9.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Locked slice reads see one state of the frame**: with locking on,[m
[32m+[m[32m  `b2nd_get_slice_cbuffer()` and `blosc2_schunk_get_slice_buffer()` hold the[m
[32m+[m[32m  shared frame lock from the refresh of the shape (counters) to the last chunk[m
[32m+[m[32m  read, and `b2nd_set_slice_cbuffer()` refreshes under its exclusive lock.[m
[32m+[m[32m  Before, every chunk access took the lock on its own, so a writer could[m
[32m+[m[32m  shrink or rewrite the frame in the middle of a read ("nchunk exceeds the[m
[32m+[m[32m  number of chunks", or chunks of different versions).  Unlocked handles are[m
[32m+[m[32m  unchanged.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Frame offsets are always compressed with typesize 8**: insert, update,[m
[32m+[m[32m  delete and reorder now pin it as append did.  Frames differ from before[m
[32m+[m[32m  only when the `BLOSC_TYPESIZE` environment variable is set (they were[m
[32m+[m[32m  readable either way).[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Uninitialized chunks read as zeros on every b2nd slice path**: reads of[m
[32m+[m[32m  part of a chunk made by `b2nd_uninit()` could return stale scratch memory,[m
[32m+[m[32m  and a partial write into such a chunk stored it around the slice.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Fixed chunks with a dictionary that could not be read back**: with[m
[32m+[m[32m  `use_dict` (LZ4, LZ4HC, ZSTD), data that did not compress was stored as is,[m
[32m+[m[32m  but the chunk kept the dictionary flag, so decompressing it failed with[m
[32m+[m[32m  `BLOSC2_ERROR_CODEC_DICT`.  Such chunks are now written without the flag,[m
[32m+[m[32m  and the ones written before are readable again.  Chunks that compress with[m
[32m+[m[32m  a dictionary are unchanged.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Fixed**: leaks on error paths of `blosc2_schunk_new()` (and of[m
[32m+[m[32m  `frame_from_schunk()` behind it), `b2nd_empty()` and friends,[m
[32m+[m[32m  `b2nd_copy()` (which also handed the freed array back), `b2nd_full()`,[m
[32m+[m[32m  `b2nd_concatenate()`, `blosc2_schunk_set_slice_buffer()` and the extension[m
[32m+[m[32m  of a b2nd array by `b2nd_resize()`; `frame_delete_chunk()` leaked the new[m
[32m+[m[32m  offsets chunk on its later error paths, and a failed realloc there lost an[m
[32m+[m[32m  in-memory frame; `blosc2_schunk_copy()` left a pointer to its own stack in[m
[32m+[m[32m  the storage it was passed; a recompressing `blosc2_schunk_copy()` padded a[m
[32m+[m[32m  shorter last chunk with the tail of the previous one; frames without chunks[m
[32m+[m[32m  `memcpy()`ed a NULL offsets chunk; a masked read that failed before[m
[32m+[m[32m  decompressing left the block mask on the context, so the next[m
[32m+[m[32m  decompression skipped blocks (also in a failed orthogonal selection, which[m
[32m+[m[32m  leaked its buffers too), and `blosc2_set_maskout()` left a freed mask there[m
[32m+[m[32m  when its allocation failed.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Fixed divide-by-zero on slice operations**: in[m
[32m+[m[32m  `blosc2_schunk_get_slice_buffer()`, `blosc2_schunk_set_slice_buffer()`,[m
[32m+[m[32m  `frame_get_slice()`, and `frame_set_slice()`, an invalid chunk with[m
[32m+[m[32m  `chunksize <= 0` caused a floating-point exception (division by zero).[m
[32m+[m[32m  These functions now validate `chunksize > 0` before computing coordinates[m
[32m+[m[32m  and safely return `BLOSC2_ERROR_INVALID_PARAM` (#834).[m
[32m+[m[32m* **CMake modernization and warning cleanup**: silenced CMake policy CMP0218[m
[32m+[m[32m  warnings and configured `CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM` to suppress compatibility[m
[32m+[m[32m  deprecation warnings for bundled third-party subprojects on CMake 3.31+.[m
