[32m+[m[32mChanges from 3.4.0 to 3.4.1[m
[32m+[m[32m===========================[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThis is a bugfix release that brings substantial performance improvements for[m
[32m+[m[32min-place writes to on-disk contiguous frames (especially on NFS), security and[m
[32m+[m[32mrobustness fixes for sparse frames, fixes for filter pipelines with 3 or more[m
[32m+[m[32mfilters alongside multithreaded DELTA decoding, and an example for sparse[m
[32m+[m[32mcoordinate handling.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Faster in-place writes to on-disk contiguous frames, especially on NFS**:[m
[32m+[m[32m  appending, inserting, updating or deleting a chunk (and so b2nd[m
[32m+[m[32m  `set_slice`) opened and closed the frame file four times and truncated it on[m
[32m+[m[32m  every trailer update.  On NFS each close is a synchronous flush to the[m
[32m+[m[32m  server, which made such writes cost about 20 ms per chunk whatever its size.[m
[32m+[m[32m  With the default filesystem backend on POSIX, a frame now keeps one writable[m
[32m+[m[32m  handle open, truncates the file only when the frame got shorter, and checks[m
[32m+[m[32m  for a replaced file with `statx(STATX_INO)` on Linux, which does not flush[m
[32m+[m[32m  pending writes.  A chunk write on NFS now costs about 1 ms of I/O; local[m
[32m+[m[32m  disks gain about 1.4x.  Other NFS clients now see the data when the kernel[m
[32m+[m[32m  writes it back, or at the latest when the frame is freed, rather than after[m
[32m+[m[32m  every update; with `BLOSC_LOCKING` it is still flushed when the lock is[m
[32m+[m[32m  released.  The writable handle comes out of the same budget as the cached[m
[32m+[m[32m  read handle (`BLOSC_MAX_CACHED_READERS`), so a schunk that has been written[m
[32m+[m[32m  to can hold two file descriptors.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Security fix for sparse (directory) frame chunk reading**:[m
[32m+[m[32m  `sframe_get_chunk()` and `frame_decompress_chunk()` now validate that the[m
[32m+[m[32m  chunk's compressed length (`cbytes`) recorded in the chunk header does not[m
[32m+[m[32m  exceed the actual size of the chunk file on disk.  Previously, a truncated or[m
[32m+[m[32m  crafted chunk file could cause an out-of-bounds heap read during decompression[m
[32m+[m[32m  (#822).  Thanks to Mar Dominguez (@mardgez).[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Fix filter pipeline buffer cycling and multithreaded DELTA decoding**:[m
[32m+[m[32m  - In `pipeline_forward()`, cycling buffers now strictly alternates between[m
[32m+[m[32m    thread-local scratch buffers so the caller's read-only input buffer is never[m
[32m+[m[32m    recycled as a destination buffer when 3 or more filters are active.[m
[32m+[m[32m    Previously, the 3rd active filter overwrote block 0 of the input buffer,[m
[32m+[m[32m    corrupting the reference block used by subsequent blocks in the DELTA filter[m
[32m+[m[32m    (#820).[m
[32m+[m[32m  - In `pipeline_backward()`, prevented destination buffer aliasing across[m
[32m+[m[32m    multiple out-of-place backward filters, ensured intermediate buffers are[m
[32m+[m[32m    copied before in-place DELTA decoding, and postponed signaling `dref`[m
[32m+[m[32m    readiness until block 0 completes its entire backward pipeline.[m
[32m+[m[32m* Added a new example demonstrating sparse coordinates storage and retrieval[m
[32m+[m[32m  (`examples/sparse_coords.c`).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mChanges from 3.3.5 to 3.4.0[m
[32m+[m[32m===========================[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThis is a minor release introducing an opt-in Blosc1-compatible chunk writer,[m
[32m+[m[32mbounded-memory APIs for variable-length block decompression, as well as[m
[32m+[m[32mimportant bug fixes and robustness improvements in DELTA filter decoding, blocksize[m
[32m+[m[32mtuning, ZFP codec handling, b2nd slicing, and thread management.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m* **New opt-in writer for Blosc1 (C-Blosc 1.x compatible) chunks**:[m
[32m+[m[32m  `blosc2_ctx_set_blosc1_format()` makes a compression context write chunks in[m
[32m+[m[32m  format version 2 with the 16-byte header, which C-Blosc 1.x can decode (stock[m
[32m+[m[32m  C-Blosc2 chunks, even with `BLOSC_BLOSC1_COMPAT`, cannot be). Besides the[m
[32m+[m[32m  header, such chunks follow the version 2 bitshuffle rule (a block is[m
[32m+[m[32m  bit-shuffled only if its number of elements is a multiple of 8) and only split[m
[32m+[m[32m  blocks that a C-Blosc 1.x reader also splits. Only plain shuffle/bitshuffle with[m
[32m+[m[32m  the blosclz, lz4, lz4hc, zlib and zstd codecs are supported; anything else[m
[32m+[m[32m  (other filters including delta, other codecs, dictionaries, variable-length[m
[32m+[m[32m  blocks) fails with an error. Off by default, no ABI change. The[m
[32m+[m[32m  `BLOSC_BLOSC1_COMPAT` environment variable now enables the same mode for[m
[32m+[m[32m  `blosc1_compress()` (it previously only dropped the extended header, still[m
[32m+[m[32m  writing a format version 5 chunk), so delta there is now an error.[m
[32m+[m[32m  `blosc2_ctx_get_blosc1_format()` reports whether a context is in this mode. The[m
[32m+[m[32m  filter may be in any slot of the pipeline; a prefilter is applied as usual.[m
[32m+[m[32m  Thanks to Mark Kittisopikul (@mkitti).[m
[32m+[m[32m* Added `blosc2_vlblock_get_nbytes_ctx()` to query one block's uncompressed size[m
[32m+[m[32m  without payload, dictionary, or scratch allocation. Lazy queries read only[m
[32m+[m[32m  the selected four-byte prefix.[m
[32m+[m[32m* Added `blosc2_vldecompress_block_into_ctx()` for single-block decompression[m
[32m+[m[32m  into a caller-owned buffer. Capacity is checked before dictionary/scratch[m
[32m+[m[32m  allocation, and scratch is sized from the admitted block. Both APIs support[m
[32m+[m[32m  ordinary and lazy VL chunks. The existing allocating API is unchanged.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Fixed DELTA filter decoding on partial chunks, maskout, and getitem**:[m
[32m+[m[32m  When an array compressed with the DELTA filter was sliced or read partially,[m
[32m+[m[32m  non-zero blocks could be corrupted because DELTA encodes blocks > 0 relative[m
[32m+[m[32m  to block 0. If block 0 was masked out or omitted (such as in `b2nd`'s compact[m
[32m+[m[32m  read path for small block fractions, or in block-level getitem), subsequent[m
[32m+[m[32m  blocks decoded against uninitialized memory. b2nd now disables the compact[m
[32m+[m[32m  path for DELTA and always unmasks block 0, and `_blosc_getitem` and[m
[32m+[m[32m  `blosc2_decompress_block_ctx` fall back to full chunk decompression when DELTA[m
[32m+[m[32m  is active. Destination pointers and sizes are reset after fallback decompression[m
[32m+[m[32m  to avoid leaving dangling references on reused contexts. Fixes Blosc/python-blosc2#740.[m
[32m+[m[32m  Thanks to Karol Gotkowski (@Karol-G) for reporting this issue.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Fixed bit-shuffled Blosc1 chunks with leftover bytes being decoded incorrectly**:[m
[32m+[m[32m  When reading a chunk with format version 2 whose bit-shuffled blocks have a[m
[32m+[m[32m  number of elements that is a multiple of 8, the bytes left over after the last[m
[32m+[m[32m  whole element were not copied to the output, so the end of the buffer was[m
[32m+[m[32m  garbage while the call reported success. They are now copied, as C-Blosc 1.x[m
[32m+[m[32m  does. See #812. Thanks to Mark Kittisopikul (@mkitti).[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Fixed chunks that could not be read back when the requested block size was not a[m
[32m+[m[32m  multiple of the typesize**: the tuner rounds a requested block size down to a multiple[m
[32m+[m[32m  of the typesize, but the chunk header recorded the size that had been asked for, so a[m
[32m+[m[32m  reader cut the chunk into blocks of a different size and failed (error -3, or wrong[m
[32m+[m[32m  data) with every filter and codec. The header now records the block size that is used.[m
[32m+[m[32m  Thanks to Mark Kittisopikul (@mkitti).[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Fixed the block size of buffers with a typesize above `BLOSC_MAX_TYPESIZE`**: such[m
[32m+[m[32m  buffers are stored as a stream of bytes with a typesize of 1, but the tuner chose the[m
[32m+[m[32m  block size with the large typesize and only then was it capped. A buffer shorter than[m
[32m+[m[32m  the typesize was split into blocks of one byte (200 bytes of typesize 300 took 1831[m
[32m+[m[32m  bytes) and a longer one got a block size that was a multiple of the large typesize.[m
[32m+[m[32m  The typesize is now capped before the tuner runs, so that it is blocked exactly like a[m
[32m+[m[32m  typesize of 1. Thanks to Mark Kittisopikul (@mkitti).[m
[32m+[m[32m* `blosc2_compress_ctx()` with a typesize of 0 stopped with SIGFPE (a division by[m
[32m+[m[32m  zero when choosing the block size). It now returns `BLOSC2_ERROR_INVALID_PARAM`.[m
[32m+[m[32m  Thanks to Mark Kittisopikul (@mkitti).[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Hardened ZFP codec against bitstream over-read and invalid metadata**:[m
[32m+[m[32m  ZFP bitstream decompression now operates from a zero-padded buffer sized to[m
[32m+[m[32m  `zfp_stream_maximum_size()`, preventing out-of-bounds heap reads when 64-bit[m
[32m+[m[32m  word reads extend past unaligned payloads (`blocks * bits` not a multiple of 64).[m
[32m+[m[32m  Also guarded against word-alignment over-read in `zfp_getcell()`, removed[m
[32m+[m[32m  redundant stream opens, validated metalayer bounds via `b2nd_deserialize_meta_inline`,[m
[32m+[m[32m  and rejected non-positive blockshapes and invalid `ndim`.[m
[32m+[m[32m  Thanks to @kalt2212 and Farkhalit Rida (@fr-manvi).[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Hardened `b2nd_get_slice_nchunks` bounds handling and allocation**:[m
[32m+[m[32m  Clamped slice bounds against negative values and `extshape`, sized `chunks_idx`[m
[32m+[m[32m  from slice bounding boxes to prevent mismatch overflows, protected against[m
[32m+[m[32m  multiplication overflow on 32-bit platforms, and handled empty slices and[m
[32m+[m[32m  shrinking reallocations without memory leaks.[m
[32m+[m[32m  Thanks to Farkhalit Rida (@fr-manvi).[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Rejected split blocks where streams do not cover blocksize**:[m
[32m+[m[32m  In `blosc_d`, decompression verifies that `neblock * nstreams == bsize` and returns[m
[32m+[m[32m  `BLOSC2_ERROR_DATA` if split streams fail to cover the full block, avoiding leaving[m
[32m+[m[32m  bytes unwritten in destination buffers (backported from C-Blosc #407).[m
[32m+[m[32m  Thanks to @suraiya-ctrl.[m
[32m+[m[32m* **Avoided unused parallel work for small block counts**:[m
[32m+[m[32m  Single-block compression and decompression jobs remain serial when `nthreads > 1`,[m
[32m+[m[32m  avoiding worker thread pool startup and overhead when threads cannot be used.[m
[32m+[m[32m  Parallel dispatch also caps worker entries to `min(nthreads, nblocks)`.[m
[32m+[m[32m  Fixed thread backend initialization on Windows. Thanks to[m
[32m+[m[32m  Johnny Kao (@johnny-kao).[m
[32m+[m[32m* Added `examples/vlchunks.c` illustrating variable-length chunk usage, updates,[m
[32m+[m[32m  compression, and verification. Thanks to Lance Uymatiao (@lanceu0128).[m
