[32m+[m[32m  Example program showing how to update many chunks of a contiguous frame[m
[32m+[m[32m  between blosc2_schunk_begin_updates() and blosc2_schunk_end_updates().[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  A contiguous frame (a cframe, on disk or in memory) stores its chunks back[m
[32m+[m[32m  to back in one payload, followed by the offsets of the chunks and a trailer:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m    | header | chunk A | chunk B | chunk C | chunk D | offsets | trailer |[m
[32m+[m[32m             <--------------- payload ---------------->[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  New data in a chunk usually compresses better or worse than the old data,[m
[32m+[m[32m  so the chunk changes size.  To keep the payload compact, an update then[m
[32m+[m[32m  moves everything stored behind the chunk.  Fine for one update, but[m
[32m+[m[32m  overwriting all n chunks one by one moves ~n^2/2 chunks.  Here chunks of 4[m
[32m+[m[32m  units grow to 5 units:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                     0    5    10   15   20[m
[32m+[m[32m    as written       AAAABBBBCCCCDDDD[m
[32m+[m[32m    update A         AAAAABBBBCCCCDDDD           B, C, D move[m
[32m+[m[32m    update B         AAAAABBBBBCCCCDDDD          C, D move[m
[32m+[m[32m    update C         AAAAABBBBBCCCCCDDDD         D moves[m
[32m+[m[32m    update D         AAAAABBBBBCCCCCDDDDD        6 chunk moves in 4 updates[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  Between blosc2_schunk_begin_updates() and blosc2_schunk_end_updates(), an[m
[32m+[m[32m  update moves no other chunk.  The new chunk goes into its old place,[m
[32m+[m[32m  together with any free space right before and after it, when it fits[m
[32m+[m[32m  there; else it goes to the end of the payload.  Whatever space is left[m
[32m+[m[32m  over stays free ('.') until blosc2_schunk_end_updates() closes all of it[m
[32m+[m[32m  in one pass, in which every chunk moves at most once.  When the chunks are[m
[32m+[m[32m  overwritten in order, the free space slides along the payload:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m                     0    5    10   15   20[m
[32m+[m[32m    as written       AAAABBBBCCCCDDDD[m
[32m+[m[32m    update A         ....BBBBCCCCDDDDAAAAA       A does not fit: to the end[m
[32m+[m[32m    update B         BBBBB...CCCCDDDDAAAAA       B fits where A and B were[m
[32m+[m[32m    update C         BBBBBCCCCC..DDDDAAAAA       the free space slides on[m
[32m+[m[32m    update D         BBBBBCCCCCDDDDD.AAAAA[m
[32m+[m[32m    end_updates()    BBBBBCCCCCDDDDDAAAAA        A moves (by 1 unit), once[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  Afterwards the payload has the same length as without the bracket; only the[m
[32m+[m[32m  order of the chunks in it can differ (A now sits at the end, as an inserted[m
[32m+[m[32m  chunk does).  That does not matter to readers: every access to a chunk goes[m
[32m+[m[32m  through its offset.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  Things to know:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  - b2nd_set_slice_cbuffer(), b2nd_set_orthogonal_selection(), b2nd_resize()[m
[32m+[m[32m    and blosc2_schunk_set_slice_buffer() bracket their own updates, so a[m
[32m+[m[32m    single slice write gets all this without doing anything.  Bracket a[m
[32m+[m[32m    sequence of such calls, or a loop over blosc2_schunk_update_chunk() or[m
[32m+[m[32m    blosc2_schunk_update_buffer() as below.[m
[32m+[m[32m  - The frame is valid at every point inside the bracket, just larger: it[m
[32m+[m[32m    can be read, and `cbytes` includes the free space until the end.[m
[32m+[m[32m  - Brackets nest; only the outermost blosc2_schunk_end_updates() closes the[m
[32m+[m[32m    free space.  A frame whose bracket is never closed stays valid, its free[m
[32m+[m[32m    space just stays (like the space of deleted chunks).[m
[32m+[m[32m  - The bracket holds the exclusive frame lock, as blosc2_schunk_lock() does[m
[32m+[m[32m    (a no-op unless file locking is enabled, see file-locking.c), so other[m
[32m+[m[32m    locked handles never see the free space.[m
[32m+[m[32m  - Only contiguous frames keep their chunks back to back.  For other[m
[32m+[m[32m    super-chunks (sparse frames, plain in-memory super-chunks) the bracket[m
[32m+[m[32m    is just the lock.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  To compile this program:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  $ gcc deferred_updates.c -o deferred_updates -lblosc2[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  To run:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  $ ./deferred_updates[m
[32m+[m[32m  Updating the chunks of a frame one by one:[m
[32m+[m[32m    as written     AAAABBBBCCCCDDDDEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHH            (1 character: 1069 bytes)[m
[32m+[m[32m    update A       AAAAABBBBCCCCDDDDEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHH           moved 29932 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update B       AAAAABBBBBCCCCDDDDEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHH          moved 25656 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update C       AAAAABBBBBCCCCCDDDDEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHH         moved 21380 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update D       AAAAABBBBBCCCCCDDDDDEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHH        moved 17104 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update E       AAAAABBBBBCCCCCDDDDDEEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHH       moved 12828 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update F       AAAAABBBBBCCCCCDDDDDEEEEEFFFFFGGGGHHHH      moved 8552 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update G       AAAAABBBBBCCCCCDDDDDEEEEEFFFFFGGGGGHHHH     moved 4276 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update H       AAAAABBBBBCCCCCDDDDDEEEEEFFFFFGGGGGHHHHH    moved 0 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    moved 119728 bytes in all, for a payload of 42432 bytes[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  The same updates inside blosc2_schunk_begin_updates() / end_updates():[m
[32m+[m[32m    as written     AAAABBBBCCCCDDDDEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHH            (1 character: 1069 bytes)[m
[32m+[m[32m    update A       ....BBBBCCCCDDDDEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHHAAAAA       moved 0 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update B       BBBBB...CCCCDDDDEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHHAAAAA       moved 0 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update C       BBBBBCCCCC..DDDDEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHHAAAAA       moved 0 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update D       BBBBBCCCCCDDDDD.EEEEFFFFGGGGHHHHAAAAA       moved 0 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update E       BBBBBCCCCCDDDDDEEEEEFFFFGGGGHHHHAAAAA       moved 0 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update F       BBBBBCCCCCDDDDDEEEEE....GGGGHHHHAAAAAFFFFF  moved 0 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update G       BBBBBCCCCCDDDDDEEEEEGGGGG...HHHHAAAAAFFFFF  moved 0 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    update H       BBBBBCCCCCDDDDDEEEEEGGGGGHHHHH..AAAAAFFFFF  moved 0 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    end_updates()  BBBBBCCCCCDDDDDEEEEEGGGGGHHHHHAAAAAFFFFF    moved 10608 bytes[m
[32m+[m[32m    (the payload shrank from 44816 to 42432 bytes)[m
[32m+[m[32m    moved 10608 bytes in all, for a payload of 42432 bytes[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  Both frames hold the same data in payloads of the same length.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m  The drawings show where the chunks really lie in the frame after every[m
[32m+[m[32m  step, one character per quarter of a chunk as first written; `moved`[m
[32m+[m[32m  counts the bytes of the other chunks that changed place.  Without the[m
[32m+[m[32m  bracket, the updates move 2.8 times the whole payload.  With it, F does not[m
[32m+[m[32m  fit into the free space either and goes to the end, G and H slide into its[m
[32m+[m[32m  old place, and blosc2_schunk_end_updates() moves just A and F, once each.[m
[32m+[m[32m  (The exact sizes depend on the codec.)[m
[32m+[m[32m*/[m
